course: medicine
lesson: 38
title: Muscles: getting stronger and getting fitter
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You have more than 600 muscles, not just the ones you show off in the mirror: they move your legs, hold up your back, let you breathe and blink, and your heart is a muscle too.
explain: Muscles get stronger by working and then repairing during rest, and the heart and lungs get fitter the same way: little and often, not one giant effort.
explain: Think of learning kick-ups: 3 on the first day, wobbly and wild; a month of practice in the garden later, 30, because your brain and muscles have learned to work together.
explain: Strength (how hard a muscle can push) and stamina (how long you can keep going) are different, and both fade if you stop, so keep moving all year, even in the holidays.
fact: The body has more than 600 muscles, and in adults muscle makes up about 40% of body weight.
fact: With regular training the heart pumps more blood per beat and the muscles grow more tiny blood vessels and get better at using oxygen.
fact: Fitness builds from many small, regular sessions, not from one huge session.
fact: Skill is the brain and muscles learning together; practice makes the nerves time each movement better.
fact: A game controller trains the thumbs, not the legs; professional footballers train most days for years and often run about 10 km in a match.
fact: Muscles grow stronger while resting after work; far too much of the same exercise every day leads to overuse pain.
fact: Coached strength training with good technique is safe for children and does not stunt growth; heavy maximum lifts are not for children.
fact: Children get plenty of protein from normal food; extra protein shakes don't build muscle by themselves.
fact: Everyone has the six-pack muscle; whether you can see it depends mostly on the fat over it and on age, and you can't burn fat from one spot with sit-ups.
fact: Strength is how much force a muscle makes; stamina is how long you can keep going; they are trained differently, and stamina is built by things like running, swimming or cycling.
fact: Fitness fades when you stop training, but it comes back faster than it took to build the first time.
fact: Before puberty, girls and boys have about the same strength, and both get stronger with practice.
exercise: Oscar: "I've only got muscles in my arms, innit." Roughly how many muscles has he got? | More than 600 | About 6 big ones | Exactly 20, one for each finger and toe | About 60,000 or more
exercise: Which of these is also a muscle? | The heart | The skull bone | The lungs, which pump the air | The fingernails and toenails
exercise: Ava jogs three times a week for six weeks. How many runs is that? | 18 | 9 | 36 | 12
exercise: Ava goes from 1 lap to 4 laps. What changed inside her? | Her heart pumps more per beat and her muscles use oxygen better | Her bones got longer and lighter, so she could run with less weight | Her lungs grew twice as big in six weeks | She grew more muscles in her arms
exercise: How does fitness build up best? | Little and often | One giant session a month | Only by being born sporty | By resting all week before a match
exercise: Arjun went from 3 kick-ups to 30 in a month. How many times as many? | 10 | 27 | 33 | 90
exercise: What made Arjun better at kick-ups? | His brain and muscles learning with practice | Being born good at football, it's in his family | Bigger arm muscles for balance | A new pair of expensive boots
exercise: Marcus plans to become a pro footballer by playing FIFA all day. What does that train? | His thumbs, not his legs | His legs and stamina, from all the tension | His heart, just like real running does | Everything a pro needs
exercise: About how far does a pro footballer often run in a match? | About 10 km | About 100 m, mostly walking | About 100 km, nearly a marathon twice | About 1 km
exercise: Leah in Belfast wants 500 push-ups a day. By Wednesday her shoulders are wrecked. Why? | Muscles grow during rest, and too much causes overuse pain | Push-ups are bad for everyone, at any age | She needed to do 1,000 to get past the pain | Shoulders can't grow muscle
exercise: Ben's uncle: "Lifting weights stunts your growth." What do experts say? | Coached strength training doesn't stunt growth | Any weight at all stops children growing | Only girls' growth is stunted | Lifting makes children grow taller and faster than others
exercise: Tyler wants protein shakes to bulk up. What's true? | Normal meals give children plenty of protein | Children can't build any muscle without shakes | Shakes alone build big muscles | Protein is bad for growing bodies
exercise: Isla does 1,000 sit-ups for a six-pack. What decides whether you see the muscle? | Mostly the fat over it and your age | The number of sit-ups you do each day, nothing else | Whether you were born with one | Eating only protein for a week
exercise: Ruben wins every arm wrestle but is wrecked in the cross-country. Why? | Strength and stamina are different things | Big muscles mean he is very fit | His arms are taking oxygen from his legs | Cross-country is unfair to strong, heavy kids
exercise: Ellie spends six weeks on the sofa. How many days off is that? | 42 | 13 | 36 | 60
exercise: Hannah does 12 push-ups, her brother 10. What's true about strength before puberty? | Girls and boys are about as strong and both improve with practice | Girls can never be as strong as boys, whatever they do | Boys are always twice as strong | Only boys get stronger with practice
test: "Bro, after six weeks of nothing I'm unfit forever, it's so mid." What's the truth? | Fitness comes back faster than it took to build | Fitness, once lost, never really comes back again | It takes ten whole years to get it back | Rest makes you fitter than training does
test: Gym influencer: "Kids, you NEED shakes for gains." Mum's reply that fits the facts? | Proper meals and lots of play are enough | Shakes are the only safe way for children to grow | Milk and eggs have no protein | Children should eat only protein
test: What would happen if Leah did 5 sets of 10 good push-ups with rest days instead? | She'd get stronger with much less risk of injury | She'd lose all her strength | She'd hurt even more than she did before | Nothing, small numbers never work
test: Lad in the yard: "Girls can't do proper push-ups anyway." Don't be mardy (sulky) about it, Hannah! What's the fact? | Strength depends on training, and girls build it just like boys | He's right, girls' arms don't get any stronger with training | Only boys' muscles grow with practice | Push-ups only work for boys
test: "You're either born sporty or you're not, sound?" Liverpool mate means "right?" What's true? | Practice changes your skill hugely | Skill is fixed at birth and can't be changed | Only tall people can really learn sport | Practice only helps adults, not children
test: Isla: "1,000 sit-ups and I'll burn my belly fat, it's giving gym bro." True? | No, you can't burn fat from one spot | Yes, sit-ups burn the fat right there | Yes, but only with 2,000 | No, sit-ups make the tummy fatter
test: In a video game, a character gets huge muscles from one potion. Real life? | Muscles grow slowly from regular work, food and sleep | One drink can build big muscles | Muscles appear overnight if you drink shakes | Muscles grow only while you train, not while you rest
test: "Shut it, weights will make you a shortie for life" — the uncle again. What would happen with coached bodyweight exercises? | He'd likely get stronger and keep growing normally | He'd stop growing at once and stay short | His bones would shrink a little every year | He'd grow wider instead of taller
test: Ruben, from Joburg: "Ag, I'll walk now now (in a bit)." Strong arms, tired legs. What should he add to his training? | Running, swimming or cycling for stamina | More arm wrestling, and nothing else | Heavier weights every single day | Nothing, strength is all he needs
test: Marcus: "Pros are just born talented, they don't even train, no cap." What's true? | Pros train most days for years | Pros never train after the age of ten | Talent means you never need to practise at all | Pros only play video games to keep sharp
test: Which plan builds fitness best for a 10-year-old? | Running and playing a few times every week | One giant run each month | Push-ups until it really hurts, every day | Resting all week, then a marathon
exam: State the approximate number of muscles present in the human body. | In excess of six hundred | Approximately six | Exactly twenty | Approximately sixty thousand
exam: Identify the principle that most accurately characterises the acquisition of physical fitness. | Regular moderate sessions sustained over time | A single session of maximal exertion each year | Inheritance alone, without any training whatsoever | Prolonged abstention from activity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning supervised strength training in childhood. | It does not impair growth when performed with correct technique | It invariably arrests the growth of the bones | It is prohibited for all persons below the age of eighteen years | It requires the consumption of protein supplements
exam: Whereas strength denotes the force a muscle can exert, ascertain the meaning of stamina. | The duration for which activity can be sustained | The maximum mass that can be lifted on a single occasion | The number of muscles in the body | The speed of a single movement
